# Architecture

Principle: **Python decides. Solidity enforces.**

The logic is programmable; the parameters are adaptive. Keep the system deterministic and auditable. Do not put the Python risk model in a smart contract.

Onchain: LP deposits/withdrawals, credit allocation, credit account, trading permissions, risk policies, trade execution, position state, utilization, repayment, interest, PnL, policy changes, events.

Offchain: historical processing, scoring, Sharpe/Sortino/drawdown, volatility/liquidity/correlation, risk modelling, simulation, credit recommendation.

Hard invariant: a trader must never execute more risk than the currently active onchain policy. Rejection happens in the contract, not the frontend.

Stack: Foundry/Solidity/OpenZeppelin on Monad; FastAPI/pandas/NumPy/Web3.py/PostgreSQL offchain; Next.js/React/TypeScript/Tailwind/wagmi/viem/ECharts frontend.

Contracts (do not implement all six at once): Registry → Vault → TradingAccount, then RiskPolicy, RiskController, Settlement.

Phase 18 execution path: Credit Account → ExecutionRouter.execute(venue, action, data) → allowlisted adapter. The router does not hold credit. Adapter 0 is the internal BTC/ETH book. Next adapters: Perpl, then Kuru. Aave supply is a later phase. Policy must include venue, action, asset, and size. Rejection stays onchain.

Events to emit: CreditIssued, CreditAdjusted, PolicyUpdated, TradeApproved, TradeRejected, RiskLevelChanged, RepaymentMade.
